     "content": "### The Verdict: **This is a strong offer. You should seriously consider accepting it, provided you are open to the move.**\n\nGoogle essentially matched your base, protected your target bonus, kept their equity intact, and gave you a **$200,000 lump-sum cash sign-on bonus**. A $200k sign-on for an L6 PM without a competing offer letter in hand is at the top of Google's band. \n\nWhen recruiters say *\"this is our best offer\"* after adding a $200k cash component, they have almost certainly hit the ceiling of their hiring committee / compensation approval limits. Further negotiations will yield diminishing returns and risk souring the relationship.\n\n---\n\n### The Numbers Breakdown\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Updated Google Offer |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | **$240,000** |\n| **Annual Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | **$48,000** (20%) |\n| **Annualized RSU Vesting** | ~$250,000 (next 12mo: $200k) | **$270,000** ($1.08M / 4) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$200,000** |\n| **Year 1 Total** | **~$476,000 \u2013 $526,000** | **~$758,000** *(or up to ~$844,000 if front-loaded)* |\n| **Year 2+ Run-Rate** | **~$526,000 + refreshes** | **~$558,000 + refreshes** |\n\n#### Why this works financially:\n1. **Year 1 Windfall:** You make an extra **~$230,000+** in your first 12 months compared to staying at Meta. That completely covers your unvested equity forfeit and gives you significant immediate liquidity.\n2. **Higher Cash Flow Going Forward:** Google\u2019s target bonus is 20% vs Meta\u2019s standard 15% at IC6, giving you an extra ~$12k/year in expected annual cash bonuses.\n3. **Parity in Long-term Equity:** The run-rate ($558k vs $526k) ensures you aren't taking a backward step in years 2 through 4.\n\n---\n\n### Critical Details to Verify Before Signing\n\nBefore you sign, confirm two specific administrative items with your recruiter:\n\n1. **Sign-on Bonus Clawback Period:**\n   * Standard Google sign-on bonuses have a **12-month clawback** (you must repay a prorated portion if you leave before 1 year). Ensure it is not structured as a 2-year clawback or split across multiple years.\n2. **Vesting Schedule:**\n   * Confirm whether the $1.08M grant vests **straight 25%/year** or on Google\u2019s **front-loaded schedule** (e.g., 33%/33%/22%/12%). If it's 33% in Year 1, your first-year equity will be **$356,400**, pushing your Year 1 total to **~$844,000**.\n\n---\n\n### Decision Framework: Take it or Stay?\n\nSince the financial gap has been resolved in Google\u2019s favor for Year 1 and parity for Year 2+, your decision now comes down to career momentum and culture:\n\n#### **Reasons to Accept:**\n* **Immediate Financial Boost:** Securing ~$750k+ in Year 1 is rare at L6 without an active bidding war.\n* **Resume & Network Diversification:** Having both Meta (IC6) and Google (L6) on your resume solidifies you as a top-tier PM across two distinct tech operating models.\n* **Work-Life Balance / Stability:** While product velocity is generally slower at Google than Meta, Google often offers better sustained work-life balance and less volatile performance-cycle pressure.\n\n#### **Reasons to Stay at Meta:**\n* **IC7 Trajectory:** If you are already performing at a high level, well-liked by leadership, and on a direct path to IC7 (Director/Principal), starting over at Google will reset your social and political capital for 12\u201318 months.\n* **PM Execution Culture:** Meta gives PMs significant direct ownership, top-down mandate speed, and engineering alignment. Google is much more consensus-driven and bottom-up; some Meta PMs find Google's organizational inertia frustrating.\n\n### Final Recommendation\nIf you have any interest in working at Google, **accept the offer**. They moved aggressively to meet your terms and solved your transition cost. If you genuinely prefer Meta's culture and are on the verge of an IC7 promotion, you can walk away knowing you extracted their top-of-band package.",
